>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `FVL_dag`

``` hol4
Term.FVL_dag : term list -> term set -> term set
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Computes the free variables of physically shared term graphs.

An invocation `FVL_dag [t1,...,tn] V` returns the same set as
`FVL [t1,...,tn] V`.  It adds the free variables of `t1,...,tn` to the
initial accumulator `V` and preserves variables already in `V`.

During one invocation, the implementation memoizes term nodes by physical
identity.  This prevents repeated traversal of a shared subterm.  Portable
physical-identity lookup has a quadratic worst-case cost in the number of
distinct nodes.  Both kernels retain an intrinsic free-variable summary for
each visited node.  The standard kernel additionally retains the free de
Bruijn indices of each node; it interprets only indices referenced by a lazy
closure body through explicit-substitution lookups, without expanding the
closure.  Summary storage can be the product of the numbers of nodes and
variables or indices.  The operation-local memo is discarded when the call
returns.

Use `FVL` for ordinary term trees.  Use `FVL_dag` when physical sharing can
make a tree traversal repeat a large subgraph.

### Failure

Never fails on well-formed terms.

### See also

[`Term.FVL`](#Term.FVL), [`Term.empty_varset`](#Term.empty_varset),
[`Term.free_vars`](#Term.free_vars)
